00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:25.340
MARK MANDEL: I think so.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:25.750
Yeah.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:27.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
excited about that, too.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:28.480
MARK MANDEL: Definitely.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:29.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But first,
let's go with the cool thing

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:31.050
of the week.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:33.720
So we're going to be talking
about Stackdriver Error

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:34.620
Reporting.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.480
So what's new in that?

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:40.150
MARK MANDEL: So as of a blog
post that came out yesterday,

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:42.840
we're pretty excited to
announce that if you need

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.620
a service to,
basically, aggregate

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:47.400
and collate the errors
that are happening

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.200
within your application, and I
guess there are probably some.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.797
I'm sure everyone
else in our audience

00:01:52.797 --> 00:01:54.630
has applications that
are completely perfect

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:55.464
and never error out.

00:01:55.464 --> 00:01:56.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Mine are perfect.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.330
MARK MANDEL: I believe so.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:01.327
So we've had log aggregation
for a really long time.

00:02:01.327 --> 00:02:03.160
You generally also want
some kind of service

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:06.681
to be, like, hey, all these
errors happen together.

00:02:06.681 --> 00:02:08.139
They're all happening
at same time.

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:09.763
Maybe they only send
you a notification

00:02:09.763 --> 00:02:12.005
when a new one pops up.

00:02:12.005 --> 00:02:13.380
It handles those
sorts of things,

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:14.660
like, where is it coming from?

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:15.690
All that sort of stuff.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:18.987
It's a lot easier to, sort
of, parse than being like,

00:02:18.987 --> 00:02:20.570
let's go through all
the logs, looking

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:22.870
for keywords, things like that.

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:24.170
It's pretty cool.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.310
And right now, it's
in beta, so, you know,

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:28.442
it comes for free,
which is pretty sweet.

00:02:28.442 --> 00:02:30.775
If you're using App Engine,
though, you get it for free.

00:02:30.775 --> 00:02:32.316
You don't have to
install any agents.

00:02:32.316 --> 00:02:34.480
You don't have to
do anything special.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.114
It's just ready to
go out of the box.

00:02:36.114 --> 00:02:37.780
If you're on a computer
engine, you just

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:39.279
have to install a
[INAUDIBLE] agent,

00:02:39.279 --> 00:02:42.457
and there are some tools
in there for sending errors

00:02:42.457 --> 00:02:43.040
through to it.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:45.300
But yeah, I think
it's pretty cool.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.380
I'm pretty excited
with how it works.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:48.296
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:02:48.296 --> 00:02:50.530
The thing that I really like
is, whenever it actually

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:55.060
finds new errors-- you can,
of course, set these up

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:57.547
to match your preferences,
but it can actually

00:02:57.547 --> 00:02:58.255
send you e-mails.

00:02:58.255 --> 00:03:01.420
So you can,
basically, be on call

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:03.880
and enjoy your life
until the moment when

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:09.360
you get the e-mail saying, well,
enjoying your life is done now.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.300
Go back to work.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:12.250
But, yeah.

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:14.790
I think it's a
very useful feature

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.330
that I hope people will use.

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:16.650
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:18.566
I mean, it's not as sexy
as some of the things

00:03:18.566 --> 00:03:20.750
that we've done in the
past, and as shiny, maybe.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:22.370
But it's, sort of, one
of those glue pieces that

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:23.410
holds everything together.

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:24.785
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
I could say it's

00:03:24.785 --> 00:03:28.847
almost as sexy and shiny
as, probably, messaging.

00:03:28.847 --> 00:03:29.930
MARK MANDEL: Like logging.

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:30.846
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:03:30.846 --> 00:03:33.141
It's like, yeah, when
it works, nobody cares.

00:03:33.141 --> 00:03:35.390
MARK MANDEL: But when you
need it, you really need it.

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:36.681
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, exactly.

00:03:36.681 --> 00:03:37.400
Cool.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.530
So I think it's time to
