00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:17.656
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:04:17.656 --> 00:04:20.214
So the playground experiences--
there is the Emotobooth.

00:04:20.214 --> 00:04:21.630
JOHN BROWN: The
Emotobooth, right.

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:24.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
There is the 360 Tour.

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:25.960
JOHN BROWN: The 360
Data Center Tour.

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:26.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
There's the BigQuery.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:28.461
JOHN BROWN: And
there's the QueryIt!.

00:04:28.461 --> 00:04:31.749
It's the BigQuery trivia game.

00:04:31.749 --> 00:04:34.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So let's
talk a little bit about them.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.825
Why don't we start
with the Emotobooth?

00:04:35.825 --> 00:04:37.450
MARK MANDEL: The
Emotobooth is awesome.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:39.640
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Tell
us, what is this exactly,

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:41.012
and how did you make it?

00:04:41.012 --> 00:04:41.720
JOHN BROWN: Sure.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:44.600
So the Emotobooth is deceptive.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.660
You think it's just going
to be a photo booth.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:47.250
You walk up.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:48.280
But all of a sudden,
the person who's

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:50.071
taking your photo isn't
just saying, smile.

00:04:50.071 --> 00:04:51.710
They're saying, frown.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:53.290
Show me some surprise.

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:54.830
Actually, show me some anger.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:55.540
What do you got?

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:56.010
You got some anger?

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:56.760
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:01.360
JOHN BROWN: And you get
through joy, sorrow, anger,

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:02.339
and surprise.

00:05:02.339 --> 00:05:03.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Isn't that the seven

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:05.294
stages of grief or something?

00:05:05.294 --> 00:05:05.512
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:05.512 --> 00:05:07.730
JOHN BROWN: Those are the
seven sins, the four sins.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:09.490
And then, after you
get your photos taken,

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:11.870
you walk around the corner,
and instead of just seeing

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:15.610
a regular photo, you get to
see a close-up of your face.

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:17.470
It shows all the
landmarks of where

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:20.780
your eyebrows are, the corners
of your eyes, your mouth.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:23.700
And then, it shows
you the sentiment.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:27.340
It gets sent through the Google
Vision, the Cloud Vision API,

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:28.640
the face detection portion.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:31.900
And it'll show the
likelihood of all

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:34.410
of the four different
sentiments that it already

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:35.240
had you go through.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:38.580
So you get to see a cool
aura surround your face.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:40.870
So if you had a very
likely joy, you're

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:44.140
gonna see a really fun
yellow halo burst around you,

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:47.630
or if you really could nail
that frown when they asked you

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:51.100
to frown, you can see a fun
blue shade that goes up.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:54.360
MARK MANDEL: I particularly
am enamored with this.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:58.200
We may have possibly changed
the title image on our Twitter

00:05:58.200 --> 00:05:59.500
account for GCP Podcast--

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:00.500
JOHN BROWN: Oh, amazing.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:02.480
MARK MANDEL: --to both
of us being surprised.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:04.063
JOHN BROWN: You guys
did a group shot?

00:06:04.063 --> 00:06:05.520
MARK MANDEL: We
did the two of us.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:06.239
It was fantastic.

00:06:06.239 --> 00:06:08.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Let
me show you right now,

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:10.222
but this is our new photo.

00:06:10.222 --> 00:06:10.930
JOHN BROWN: Yeah.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:11.670
That's a good one.

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:11.950
Yeah.

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:13.908
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So you
can find that if you go

00:06:13.908 --> 00:06:18.240
to Twitter.com/GCPPodcast,
you will see both of us being

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:19.390
incredibly surprised.

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:21.556
MARK MANDEL: One thing I
love, and you do this well,

00:06:21.556 --> 00:06:24.020
is not only did you
let people see it

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:26.780
on the images around
the corner, but also,

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:29.380
when you tweet it out, you've
got a gist there with, like,

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:32.207
all the data from Cloud Vision
API as well as all the images

00:06:32.207 --> 00:06:33.706
that you took in
the photos as well.

00:06:33.706 --> 00:06:34.260
JOHN BROWN: Exactly.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:34.760
Yeah.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:36.651
So you can explore the
photo and all the data

00:06:36.651 --> 00:06:37.525
that's related to it.

00:06:37.525 --> 00:06:38.280
It's all in one place.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:39.730
You can, sort of,
compare and contrast

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:41.000
between the different pieces.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:41.400
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:42.524
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:06:42.524 --> 00:06:43.490
I really liked it.

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:46.110
The second one that a lot of
people were very excited about

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:47.342
is the 360 Tour.

00:06:47.342 --> 00:06:48.050
JOHN BROWN: Yeah.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:54.610
So our company did the
planning of the actual 360

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:55.510
portion of it.

00:06:55.510 --> 00:06:57.620
We didn't actually do the video.

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:01.067
A Google team did the internal
data center video for it.

00:07:01.067 --> 00:07:03.650
But we, sort of, integrated it
with the rest of the playground

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:04.150
experience.

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:06.270
People would come
in, look at it,

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.640
get to see the
behind-the-scenes of the server,

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:11.200
that Google is finally
letting people see,

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:12.850
in a really cool way.

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:14.570
You get to see it
in-- I don't know,

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:16.246
it's probably,
about, 30 feet wide.

00:07:16.246 --> 00:07:17.620
You can actually
feel like you're

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:18.870
standing inside of the server.

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:20.453
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It
is pretty amazing.

00:07:20.453 --> 00:07:22.410
I have personally
been at a data center,

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:24.300
and this is probably
the closest you can

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:25.660
get without working at Google.

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:25.920
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:27.930
I was pretty impressed
with the amount of stuff

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:28.820
that we're actually showing
inside the data center.

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:28.940
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:29.648
JOHN BROWN: Yeah.

00:07:29.648 --> 00:07:30.570
It's really neat.

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:31.486
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:07:31.486 --> 00:07:32.170
Very cool.

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:36.050
One more question about the
last demo that we have is,

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:38.105
the BigQuery-- called, QueryIt!?

00:07:38.105 --> 00:07:38.980
JOHN BROWN: QueryIt!.

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:40.565
QueryIt, exclamation point.

00:07:40.565 --> 00:07:41.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
I love the name.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:43.630
MARK MANDEL: I haven't had
a chance to play with this.

00:07:43.630 --> 00:07:45.050
So tell me about
what's the deal there.

00:07:45.050 --> 00:07:45.758
JOHN BROWN: Yeah.

00:07:45.758 --> 00:07:48.290
So essentially, what we did
is, we wrote 200 questions.

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:50.600
They're, sort of, trivia
questions-- things like,

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:53.980
what state mentions Velveeta
cheese the most on Reddit?

00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:59.950
Or what musician's Wikipedia
page was visited most in 2015?

00:07:59.950 --> 00:08:03.410
And what we've done is, we
have a ton of public data

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:07.050
sets for Reddit,
Wikipedia, GDELT,

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:11.700
and a couple others that we
have written queries for,

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:13.460
that when you type
in your answer,

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:16.960
it actually puts it into the
query, runs the query live.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:18.390
There's a really
fun visualization

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:21.470
that has a visualized
model of what's happening

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:24.110
behind the scenes in BigQuery.

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:25.650
So it's not 100%
accurate, but it's

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:27.677
a really cool visual
representation

00:08:27.677 --> 00:08:28.510
of what's happening.

00:08:28.510 --> 00:08:29.590
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It
looks really cool.

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:29.750
JOHN BROWN: Yeah.

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:31.883
And then, you get points
based on the accuracy

00:08:31.883 --> 00:08:32.549
of your results.

00:08:32.549 --> 00:08:34.159
And there are two
podiums, so you get

00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:35.770
to play a friend or a stranger.

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:37.270
There's a little
bit of competition.

00:08:37.270 --> 00:08:39.669
Maybe you can talk smack to
each other, back and forth,

00:08:39.669 --> 00:08:41.000
but it's a lot of fun.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:42.799
MARK MANDEL: So you're,
like, basically,

