00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:46.380
Tell us a little bit
about your experience

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:48.470
using the Cloud to
build these demos.

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:49.512
How was it?

00:09:49.512 --> 00:09:50.220
JOHN BROWN: Sure.

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:53.690
So one of the really
interesting things

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:56.740
is, we got to use the Cloud
Vision API when it was still

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:57.920
in the alpha stage.

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:00.200
So we got to see what
it was going to be,

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:03.000
and we got to try all the
different features early on.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.860
And as we were developing
the Emotobooth,

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:09.180
we got to try things like the
object detection or the label

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:10.100
detection.

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:11.570
We got to try the OCR.

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:13.732
We got to try landmarks.

00:10:13.732 --> 00:10:15.440
We got to try all
these different things,

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:17.910
and we got to craft the
experience that we ended up

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:22.330
seeing, based on being able to
easily put all the images in,

00:10:22.330 --> 00:10:25.277
run through it, get a bunch of
that data, and, sort of, like,

00:10:25.277 --> 00:10:27.610
look at it, and figure out
what we wanted to do with it.

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:30.220
And we ended up going with
the sentiment analysis,

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:33.989
but we had things-- like, we
had giant cutouts of hot dogs,

00:10:33.989 --> 00:10:35.780
and we're, like, can
we detect the hot dog?

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:38.490
We wanted to give people cowboy
hats and put them into a scene,

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:40.250
and is it going to
detect the cowboy hats?

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:41.270
We tried a bunch of
different things,

00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:43.470
but the Cloud Vision API
was really great, even

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:44.720
in the alpha stage.

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:47.730
And then, the only thing that--
it only got better as it went.

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:49.910
We got to run more
queries on it,

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:52.180
we got to run more
hits on the API,

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:55.740
and it was a lot of fun to
develop a physical experience

00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:56.470
around it.

00:10:56.470 --> 00:10:57.428
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:10:57.428 --> 00:10:59.900
And you found it, I'm guessing,
like, a relatively easy API

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:00.350
to use.

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:00.610
Like, it wasn't difficult.

00:11:00.610 --> 00:11:01.680
JOHN BROWN: Yeah, absolutely.

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:02.179
Yeah.

00:11:02.179 --> 00:11:05.760
We built a tool to just
drag and drop images

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:07.900
into a web browser
in, about, half a day.

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:10.191
And we were up and running,
and we could see the output

00:11:10.191 --> 00:11:12.580
and zoom in on the faces
where they were detected,

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:14.160
and get all the
sentiment analysis.

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:15.868
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Is
that tool ope source?

00:11:15.868 --> 00:11:19.040
JOHN BROWN: So all of the tools
are going to be open-source,

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:21.010
probably, about, mid-April.

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:22.720
We rushed to get
everything here.

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:24.950
We've got a little bit technical
debt we need to take care of.

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:25.342
MARK MANDEL: I know
this very well.

00:11:25.342 --> 00:11:26.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I can imagine.

00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:26.460
Yeah.

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:27.080
We've been there.

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:27.410
JOHN BROWN: But, yeah.

00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:29.820
All the tools and the
Emotobooth will be up.

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:31.640
We're going to be,
hopefully, going to IO,

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:34.726
so if anybody didn't get
to come and experience

00:11:34.726 --> 00:11:36.100
