FRANCESC CAMPOY: So we're
now with Violet Pena, one

00:12:55.620 --> 00:12:58.150
of the developers
from Instrument,

00:12:58.150 --> 00:13:00.960
the company that created
all of these cool demos that

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:02.330
are surrounding us.

00:13:02.330 --> 00:13:02.960
Hi, Violet.

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:03.710
How are you doing?

00:13:03.710 --> 00:13:04.930
VIOLET PENA: Hi I'm great.

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:05.615
How are you guys doing?

00:13:05.615 --> 00:13:06.823
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Doing great.

00:13:06.823 --> 00:13:08.570
Very excited to have you here.

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:10.865
Yesterday, we got to
interview John Brown.

00:13:10.865 --> 00:13:13.640
He told us a little
bit about all the demos

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:15.390
from a high point of view.

00:13:15.390 --> 00:13:17.400
What demos did you work on?

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:20.420
VIOLET PENA: So I was focused,
just on the Emotobooth,

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:22.235
using the Vision API.

00:13:22.235 --> 00:13:25.284
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool--
which was an amazing demo.

00:13:25.284 --> 00:13:26.575
MARK MANDEL: We love this demo.

00:13:26.575 --> 00:13:26.860
VIOLET PENA: Great.

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:27.776
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:13:27.776 --> 00:13:28.540
Lots of fun.

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:30.680
Tell us a little bit,
how was the experience?

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:33.520
How did you come up with the
idea and how did it work?

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:34.270
VIOLET PENA: Yeah.

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:37.760
So we were looking for something
to showcase the Google Cloud

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:39.440
Vision API.

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:41.650
And we were looking
for something

00:13:41.650 --> 00:13:44.240
that we could fit into
the conference experience

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:46.840
as, like, a really
fun, you know, break

00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:52.560
from more technical sessions
and those kinds of experiences.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:54.350
So we were trying
to figure out how

00:13:54.350 --> 00:13:58.210
to highlight its
different capabilities

00:13:58.210 --> 00:14:01.280
or how to make that
fun and interactive.

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:04.610
And so we ended up
landing on looking

00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:08.330
at facial recognition,
which is one

00:14:08.330 --> 00:14:11.130
of the attributes
of the Vision API.

00:14:11.130 --> 00:14:14.120
And so we started getting
really great results

00:14:14.120 --> 00:14:18.540
with facial recognition
and sentiment analysis.

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:21.510
And so then, we ended
up with this photo booth

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:24.760
that you walk into.

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:26.580
We help you make
different faces,

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:29.030
conjure up different emotions.

00:14:29.030 --> 00:14:33.050
And then, we analyze
the points on your face

00:14:33.050 --> 00:14:34.980
and your different
anatomical features,

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:40.970
and then we tell you
about what emotions you're

00:14:40.970 --> 00:14:42.579
displaying in that photo.

00:14:42.579 --> 00:14:44.870
MARK MANDEL: So was there a
particular part of the demo

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:46.520
that you specifically worked on?

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:47.270
VIOLET PENA: Yeah.

00:14:47.270 --> 00:14:50.200
I was focused on the front end.

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:53.930
So everything that you
see in the displays,

00:14:53.930 --> 00:14:55.450
I had a large hand in.

00:14:55.450 --> 00:14:59.230
And then, I have
some coworkers who

00:14:59.230 --> 00:15:02.680
are more focused on the
back-end and infrastructure

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:03.340
sides of stuff.

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:08.240
And so that was vital
to actually kicking off

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:13.770
all the analysis and experience
after the photo is taken.

00:15:13.770 --> 00:15:15.560
So that was hooking
up the camera

00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:18.680
to getting everything
to run properly

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:20.440
and getting all the
back-end processing

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:23.874
to happen so that we can get
your beautiful photo to you.

00:15:23.874 --> 00:15:25.790
MARK MANDEL: So you did
a lot of the animation

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:27.440
and, like, the display
up on the screen

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:29.148
that you actually see
as you walk around?

00:15:29.148 --> 00:15:29.960
VIOLET PENA: Yeah.

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:30.660
MARK MANDEL: That's awesome.

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:33.118
VIOLET PENA: There was a lot
of just cycling back and forth

00:15:33.118 --> 00:15:35.300
between me and the
designers about, oh, well,

00:15:35.300 --> 00:15:38.170
what if we tweak this
color and that opacity,

00:15:38.170 --> 00:15:40.320
and change those timings?

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:42.020
And so it was really,
really amazing.

00:15:42.020 --> 00:15:43.186
MARK MANDEL: It looks great.

00:15:43.186 --> 00:15:44.890
It looks really, really great.

00:15:44.890 --> 00:15:46.681
FRANCESC CAMPOY: There's
a couple of things

00:15:46.681 --> 00:15:48.040
that I really like.

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:50.720
One of them is, kind of,
subtle, but actually-- so

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:52.880
that aura that surrounds
you surrounds only the

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:55.070
faces that you can actually see.

00:15:55.070 --> 00:15:55.820
VIOLET PENA: Yeah.

00:15:55.820 --> 00:15:57.060
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
the picture with you

00:15:57.060 --> 00:15:59.570
and three more people-- I
think you were in that picture.

00:15:59.570 --> 00:16:00.390
Maybe not.

00:16:00.390 --> 00:16:02.690
But there were four
girls in the picture,

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:04.210
and only one is looking forward.

00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:05.230
The others are
looking backwards.

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:05.760
VIOLET PENA: Yeah.

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:06.320
Oh, yeah.

00:16:06.320 --> 00:16:07.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
[INAUDIBLE] highlight

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:09.160
of that single person
looking at the camera.

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:10.730
How does that work?

00:16:10.730 --> 00:16:14.030
VIOLET PENA: So the way that
we've had the experience work

00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:17.980
is that we want this colorful
visual treatment on the photo,

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:20.260
but we didn't want to
cover up people's faces.

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:23.044
And so that's how we were also
bringing in the hexagon motif

00:16:23.044 --> 00:16:24.210
into the whole thing, right?

00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:26.910
Draw a hexagon around the
faces that you find, but then,

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:29.270
of course, it only
finds one face.

00:16:29.270 --> 00:16:33.190
It doesn't know if it's a person
if it can't see your face.

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:35.994
So that's how, sometimes, we
ended up with, like you saw,

00:16:35.994 --> 00:16:37.410
the three people
are turned around

00:16:37.410 --> 00:16:38.910
and one person is
facing forward.

00:16:38.910 --> 00:16:39.950
That's the only face it finds.

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:41.616
So it only draws a
hexagon around there,

00:16:41.616 --> 00:16:45.630
and it analyzes the
emotions from that one face.

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:47.350
So we actually also
had some group shots

00:16:47.350 --> 00:16:49.992
where one person
wouldn't be ready,

00:16:49.992 --> 00:16:52.450
and so just totally by accident,
they would be looking away

00:16:52.450 --> 00:16:55.334
or-- in our testing,
someone was sneezing

00:16:55.334 --> 00:16:57.500
while the photo was taken,
and so her head was down.

00:16:57.500 --> 00:16:59.710
And so it drew a
hexagon around, like,

00:16:59.710 --> 00:17:02.180
the other people in the
picture, and then she was, like,

00:17:02.180 --> 00:17:03.340
covered up by the color.

00:17:03.340 --> 00:17:03.870
Yeah.

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:05.170
Yeah, so [LAUGHTER]
that happens.

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:06.586
MARK MANDEL: But
no, I really like

00:17:06.586 --> 00:17:09.500
that as, like-- it's just a
really cool idea for using

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:11.510
Vision API for, like,
being able to recognize,

00:17:11.510 --> 00:17:13.907
OK, these are the things
that we want to focus on.

00:17:13.907 --> 00:17:14.990
And so here are the faces.

00:17:14.990 --> 00:17:17.210
So we're going to draw
around that and frame that,

00:17:17.210 --> 00:17:18.613
because we know where
they are in the picture.

00:17:18.613 --> 00:17:19.363
VIOLET PENA: Yeah.

00:17:19.363 --> 00:17:22.569
Something else really funny that
happened with face detection,

00:17:22.569 --> 00:17:25.700
though-- and this wasn't
a bug-- kind of a bug,

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:28.250
but it was just really,
really funny to me.

00:17:28.250 --> 00:17:30.950
Some people here
have security badges

00:17:30.950 --> 00:17:32.377
with photos of them on it.

00:17:32.377 --> 00:17:33.460
MARK MANDEL: Oh. [CHUCKLE]

00:17:33.460 --> 00:17:35.626
VIOLET PENA: And so it
didn't happen to all of them,

00:17:35.626 --> 00:17:37.990
but to, maybe,
about half of them.

00:17:37.990 --> 00:17:40.524
I guess the light
made it clear enough.

00:17:40.524 --> 00:17:41.940
Like, the flash
was bright enough,

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:44.660
the badges angled forwards,
so it would actually

00:17:44.660 --> 00:17:46.300
detect the face on their badge.

00:17:46.300 --> 00:17:47.860
And, like, these
are really little.

00:17:47.860 --> 00:17:48.090
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:48.881
VIOLET PENA: Right?

00:17:48.881 --> 00:17:49.780
Like, a little photo.

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:50.280
Yeah.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:54.260
And so most of the people--
it would map-- like,

00:17:54.260 --> 00:17:55.800
there is another
face in the photo.

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:57.480
Then, it would zoom
in on that face,

00:17:57.480 --> 00:18:00.430
and it was, like, this little
blurry picture on a card.

00:18:00.430 --> 00:18:02.360
And so someone was
actually smiling

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:04.530
really big in her
photo on her badge,

00:18:04.530 --> 00:18:08.000
and it actually kept
saying, joy, very likely,

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:08.686
for that badge.

00:18:08.686 --> 00:18:09.206
MARK MANDEL: Oh, wow.

00:18:09.206 --> 00:18:09.380
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:11.338
VIOLET PENA: So it was
even sentiment-detecting

00:18:11.338 --> 00:18:13.380
in that teeny badged photo.

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:16.846
So I think it's hilarious
when stuff like that happens.

00:18:16.846 --> 00:18:18.262
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
I really like it.

00:18:18.262 --> 00:18:19.490
Cool.

00:18:19.490 --> 00:18:21.240
John also mentioned
that apparently, there

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:23.964
were hot dogs involved.

00:18:23.964 --> 00:18:26.380
Could you tell us a little bit
more about what's going on?

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:27.150
VIOLET PENA: Yeah.

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:31.700
So you're not going to see any
hot dogs in the product today.

00:18:31.700 --> 00:18:34.800
But that was part of the
journey as we were figuring out

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:36.280
what the experience should be.

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:39.140
So, kind of, close to the
beginning, we were, like, well,

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:40.550
a photo booth would be
great, because, you know,

00:18:40.550 --> 00:18:41.841
conferences have a photo booth.

00:18:41.841 --> 00:18:43.487
But, like, we're
analyzing photos,

00:18:43.487 --> 00:18:44.820
so let's do something with that.

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:47.870
So we were trying to figure out
what the photo booth should do,

00:18:47.870 --> 00:18:52.420
and the Vision API has
so many options of things

00:18:52.420 --> 00:18:55.020
that you can try to analyze.

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:57.750
And so one of the options
is label detection,

00:18:57.750 --> 00:19:00.920
and it can tell you what
a photo is of, in general.

00:19:00.920 --> 00:19:03.810
So, like, you pass
in a photo of a farm,

00:19:03.810 --> 00:19:07.990
and it will give you
labels about what

00:19:07.990 --> 00:19:10.620
it would classify the photo
as, in confidence scores.

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:12.971
And so we were trying
to leverage that.

00:19:12.971 --> 00:19:14.470
And so we were
thinking, like, well,

00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:16.360
what if we gave people props?

00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:20.310
And then, we could
get labels going.

00:19:20.310 --> 00:19:25.055
So like, if you have a hot
dog, for instance, then--

00:19:25.055 --> 00:19:26.680
MARK MANDEL: Which
is something I often

00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:27.804
do-- I just have a hot dog.

00:19:27.804 --> 00:19:28.750
VIOLET PENA: Yeah.

00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:31.180
So we were printing
out foam core props.

00:19:31.180 --> 00:19:33.997
This was, like, a-- however
many-- four-foot-long hot dogs,

00:19:33.997 --> 00:19:34.580
or something--

00:19:34.580 --> 00:19:36.170
FRANCESC CAMPOY: There
were no real hot dogs?

00:19:36.170 --> 00:19:36.836
VIOLET PENA: No.

00:19:36.836 --> 00:19:39.580
No actual, because those
would be a little bit small.

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:43.490
But we were printing out,
like, a huge dog, a boom box--

00:19:43.490 --> 00:19:46.734
we were trying to do
landmarks, so we had a sphinx--

00:19:46.734 --> 00:19:47.650
MARK MANDEL: Oh, cool.

00:19:47.650 --> 00:19:49.941
VIOLET PENA: --this big,
Eiffel Tower, stuff like that.

00:19:49.941 --> 00:19:51.760
So we were trying
to do that, maybe.

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:54.750
So if you have a hot dog
or, like, a slice of pizza,

00:19:54.750 --> 00:19:56.870
maybe it'll change the
background of the photo

00:19:56.870 --> 00:20:00.350
to put you and your
group in a restaurant,

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:01.490
or something like that.

00:20:01.490 --> 00:20:02.989
So we were playing
around with that,

00:20:02.989 --> 00:20:08.310
and it just got too hairy,
because as of right now,

00:20:08.310 --> 00:20:10.650
anyway, it doesn't
detect specific objects

00:20:10.650 --> 00:20:13.130
and say, like, oh,
I see a hot dog at,

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:15.280
you know, this
pixel by that pixel.

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:16.334
MARK MANDEL: Oh, yeah.

00:20:16.334 --> 00:20:18.500
VIOLET PENA: It just tells
you what the whole image,

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:19.250
in general, is of.

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:19.999
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:20:19.999 --> 00:20:22.544
VIOLET PENA: So if you send
it the whole image of people,

00:20:22.544 --> 00:20:23.960
even if they're
holding a hot dog,

00:20:23.960 --> 00:20:28.070
it'll say the top
guess is group setting

00:20:28.070 --> 00:20:29.920
or-- photo shoot,
actually, was the guess

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:30.630
it was giving us a lot.

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:32.754
And we were, like, oh,
that's not very interesting.

00:20:32.754 --> 00:20:33.830
Come on.

00:20:33.830 --> 00:20:37.570
So yeah, we were trying
to slice up the image

00:20:37.570 --> 00:20:39.410
and send through little parts.

00:20:39.410 --> 00:20:43.200
But it was taking a
long time, because we

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:45.280
had to keep sending
more small images

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:47.017
and checking those results.

00:20:47.017 --> 00:20:48.850
Yeah and so at some
point we were just like,

00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:51.016
oh, I don't know if this
is going to work right now.

00:20:51.016 --> 00:20:53.000
But then, we had such
other amazing stuff

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:56.970
to dig into that, I think,
ended up being really, really

00:20:56.970 --> 00:20:57.574
successful.

00:20:57.574 --> 00:20:59.240
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It
is a very cool demo.

00:20:59.240 --> 00:21:01.750
Are there any other ideas
that you would like to share?

00:21:01.750 --> 00:21:03.890
Like, oh, yeah, we
thought about this-- other

00:21:03.890 --> 00:21:06.140
than the hot dogs-- and it
did not work, or something.

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:07.389
VIOLET PENA: [LAUGHTER] Right.

00:21:07.389 --> 00:21:08.262
Yeah.

00:21:08.262 --> 00:21:09.720
Like I said, we
also had landmarks.

00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:12.136
And so something that we were
throwing around a little bit

00:21:12.136 --> 00:21:15.400
was-- so if you
have landmarks, we

00:21:15.400 --> 00:21:17.634
wanted to almost make
postcard treatments.

00:21:17.634 --> 00:21:18.550
MARK MANDEL: Oh, cool.

00:21:18.550 --> 00:21:24.970
VIOLET PENA: So like if you're
walking around with the Eiffel

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:28.910
Tower and it's in your photo,
then the visual treatment

00:21:28.910 --> 00:21:32.010
we do-- it puts the city
of Paris behind you,

00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:34.765
and then we have a "Greetings
from Paris" across the front.

00:21:34.765 --> 00:21:35.515
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:21:35.515 --> 00:21:35.920
I like that.

00:21:35.920 --> 00:21:37.250
VIOLET PENA: So we
were doing that,

00:21:37.250 --> 00:21:38.625
and we were trying
to figure out,

00:21:38.625 --> 00:21:42.060
like, OK, so if one person
has the Statue of Liberty,

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:46.110
and one person has the Arc de
Triomphe, what happens then?

00:21:46.110 --> 00:21:47.280
Where are we putting them?

00:21:47.280 --> 00:21:48.650
In what country and what city?

00:21:48.650 --> 00:21:48.820
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:21:48.820 --> 00:21:49.190
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:50.231
VIOLET PENA: So we were--

00:21:50.231 --> 00:21:51.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Vegas? [LAUGHTER]

00:21:51.730 --> 00:21:52.490
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, yeah.

00:21:52.490 --> 00:21:53.530
I like that-- that's good.

00:21:53.530 --> 00:21:55.860
VIOLET PENA: That's actually--
we never thought of that.

00:21:55.860 --> 00:21:56.360
That could have been our
default for when you're

00:21:56.360 --> 00:21:57.730
mixing really weird stuff.

00:21:57.730 --> 00:21:58.850
MARK MANDEL: (LAUGHING)
It's just Vegas.

00:21:58.850 --> 00:22:00.880
VIOLET PENA: Yeah,
probably Vegas. [LAUGHTER]

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:03.970
So that was really fun.

00:22:03.970 --> 00:22:07.241
That was another route
that we, kind of, started

00:22:07.241 --> 00:22:08.490
going down, playing with that.

00:22:08.490 --> 00:22:10.525
Or maybe, like, we layer
backgrounds together

00:22:10.525 --> 00:22:13.242
or we combine city names,
like, "Welcome to New Paris,"

00:22:13.242 --> 00:22:14.200
or something like that.

00:22:14.200 --> 00:22:16.240
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, that
would be really cool.

00:22:16.240 --> 00:22:18.460
VIOLET PENA: I mean, that
would have been really fun.

00:22:18.460 --> 00:22:21.251
Maybe we'll do some of that,
just as a little fun side

00:22:21.251 --> 00:22:21.750
project.

00:22:21.750 --> 00:22:23.208
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That'd be awesome.

00:22:23.208 --> 00:22:25.770
Let's see what you
build for GCP next year.

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:26.520
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:27.080
VIOLET PENA: Yeah.

00:22:27.080 --> 00:22:28.660
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I'm
very excited about that.

00:22:28.660 --> 00:22:29.350
MARK MANDEL: This
has been awesome.

00:22:29.350 --> 00:22:30.010
Thank you so much.

00:22:30.010 --> 00:22:30.650
JOHN BROWN: Thank you.

00:22:30.650 --> 00:22:31.790
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Thank you
so much for taking the time

00:22:31.790 --> 00:22:32.590
to talk to us.

00:22:32.590 --> 00:22:32.850
VIOLET PENA: Yeah.

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:33.060
MARK MANDEL: Cheers.

00:22:33.060 --> 00:22:33.850
VIOLET PENA: Thanks I having me.

00:22:33.850 --> 00:22:33.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Thank you.

00:22:33.980 --> 00:22:34.688
VIOLET PENA: Bye.

00:22:34.688 --> 00:22:37.149
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So we're here
again with, again, someone

00:22:37.149 --> 00:22:39.190
working for Instrument,
this amazing company that

00:22:39.190 --> 00:22:42.760
created the very cool demos
that are surrounding us today

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:44.340
at GCP Next.

00:22:44.340 --> 00:22:46.017
We have Nick
Tzaperas-- Tzaperas?

00:22:46.017 --> 00:22:46.850
NICK TZAPERAS: Yeah.

00:22:46.850 --> 00:22:47.766
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:22:47.766 --> 00:22:52.710
Cool. [CHUCKLE] He worked
specifically on the QueryIt!

00:22:52.710 --> 00:22:53.290
demo, right?

00:22:53.290 --> 00:22:53.610
NICK TZAPERAS: Yeah.

00:22:53.610 --> 00:22:54.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Hi, Nick.

00:22:54.250 --> 00:22:54.930
How are you doing?

00:22:54.930 --> 00:22:55.475
NICK TZAPERAS: Doing good.

00:22:55.475 --> 00:22:57.891
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Could you
tell us a little bit about you,

00:22:57.891 --> 00:23:00.300
what you do at
Instrument, and then

00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:03.720
how is it that someone came
up with this cool idea?

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:04.910
NICK TZAPERAS: Yeah.

00:23:04.910 --> 00:23:08.564
Well, at Instrument, I'm a
back-end developer and, kind

00:23:08.564 --> 00:23:09.480
of all over the place.

00:23:09.480 --> 00:23:11.940
We have a lot of front-end
talent at Instrument,

00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:14.190
a lot of graphic designers
and front-end developers.

00:23:14.190 --> 00:23:19.470
So anything that's at
all dev-ops or writing

00:23:19.470 --> 00:23:22.170
SQL for BigQuery, that
kind of back-end thing--

00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:24.560
I'd hop on that.

00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:27.400
And I think the
idea for BigQuery

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:33.430
came from our creative
talent at Instrument,

00:23:33.430 --> 00:23:38.480
and just trying to mimic
the Family Feud style,

00:23:38.480 --> 00:23:43.140
like, create a
game for BigQuery.

00:23:43.140 --> 00:23:44.920
That's the team that
I work on, and that's

00:23:44.920 --> 00:23:47.430
what we try to do-- create
interactive experiences

00:23:47.430 --> 00:23:48.920
with technology.

00:23:48.920 --> 00:23:50.366
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:23:50.366 --> 00:23:52.930
So you mentioned that you
are a back-end engineer.

00:23:52.930 --> 00:23:55.210
Have you ever
written SQL before?

00:23:55.210 --> 00:23:57.550
Is that something that you
put in your resume, or--

00:23:57.550 --> 00:23:58.746
NICK TZAPERAS: Yeah.

00:23:58.746 --> 00:24:00.765
I've definitely
run a lot of SQL.

00:24:00.765 --> 00:24:02.806
A few jobs ago, I was
actually somewhat of a DVA.

00:24:02.806 --> 00:24:04.970
It was a small company.

00:24:04.970 --> 00:24:07.190
So there was a lot of things
that needed to be done.

00:24:07.190 --> 00:24:10.460
And I was running
backups for the database,

00:24:10.460 --> 00:24:13.390
running migrations,
and writing SQL

00:24:13.390 --> 00:24:16.250
to try to figure out why the
database was running slow,

00:24:16.250 --> 00:24:18.420
trying to do performance
optimizations on it.

00:24:18.420 --> 00:24:21.670
So pretty familiar with SQL,
going into the BigQuery.

00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:22.830
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:26.110
Did you need to do any of
those, like, making sure

00:24:26.110 --> 00:24:28.494
that queries are optimized
and stuff, with BigQuery?

00:24:28.494 --> 00:24:29.660
NICK TZAPERAS: A little bit.

00:24:29.660 --> 00:24:30.820
Yeah.

00:24:30.820 --> 00:24:32.700
We were trying to make
an interactive game,

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:34.824
and, you know, any good
interactive experience

00:24:34.824 --> 00:24:37.490
won't have you sitting there for
a minute, waiting for the query

00:24:37.490 --> 00:24:37.990
to finish.

00:24:37.990 --> 00:24:42.340
And we're trying to
support a lot of features,

00:24:42.340 --> 00:24:47.600
so-- some questions like,
what was the most talked about

00:24:47.600 --> 00:24:50.370
rapper on /r/music
in the last year?

00:24:50.370 --> 00:24:54.420
We wanted people to be able to
type in Kanye or Kanye West,

00:24:54.420 --> 00:25:00.140
or J dash Z or space Z,
and have it all work.

00:25:00.140 --> 00:25:03.730
And so the databases that
we were using had aliases,

00:25:03.730 --> 00:25:05.910
and trying to pull
in all the aliases

00:25:05.910 --> 00:25:11.055
for every single musician
and searching Reddit for that

00:25:11.055 --> 00:25:12.460
was taking, like, a minute.

00:25:12.460 --> 00:25:14.342
And the game's slow
in chugging along,

00:25:14.342 --> 00:25:15.850
because you type in your
answer and you're sitting there

00:25:15.850 --> 00:25:17.620
for a minute, just
waiting and waiting.

00:25:17.620 --> 00:25:21.560
And the BigQuery
interface actually

00:25:21.560 --> 00:25:24.030
had some-- like, when
you run your query,

00:25:24.030 --> 00:25:26.330
it has an explanation
of where time was spent

00:25:26.330 --> 00:25:30.520
and shows you portions of your
query that took a long time.

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:34.850
And I was able to use that to
figure out what to, kind of,

00:25:34.850 --> 00:25:39.010
cut out of the query to make
it run faster, to make it

00:25:39.010 --> 00:25:41.809
a faster-paced trivia game.

00:25:41.809 --> 00:25:43.600
MARK MANDEL: Was this
your first experience

00:25:43.600 --> 00:25:44.640
working with BigQuery?

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:45.000
NICK TZAPERAS: Oh, yeah.

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:45.610
Definitely.

00:25:45.610 --> 00:25:46.214
Just-- yeah.

00:25:46.214 --> 00:25:47.880
MARK MANDEL: But
pretty easy to pick up?

00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:49.380
It wasn't difficult in any way?

00:25:49.380 --> 00:25:50.213
NICK TZAPERAS: Yeah.

00:25:50.213 --> 00:25:51.600
I just jumped into
the interface.

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:53.630
I think almost
the entire time, I

00:25:53.630 --> 00:25:56.015
was just using the
interface that was created.

00:25:56.015 --> 00:26:00.705
A coworker of mine--
eventually, we got up to-- we

00:26:00.705 --> 00:26:02.330
were trying to create
as many questions

00:26:02.330 --> 00:26:03.700
as possible, because
that's something else.

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:05.783
We want people to be able
to stick around and play

00:26:05.783 --> 00:26:08.666
a few times and not see the
same questions over and over.

00:26:08.666 --> 00:26:11.012
So we were shooting
for 200 questions.

00:26:11.012 --> 00:26:12.109
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:26:12.109 --> 00:26:14.400
VIOLET PENA: So eventually,
once we got up to that mini

00:26:14.400 --> 00:26:17.680
[INAUDIBLE]-- just a huge
database of questions,

00:26:17.680 --> 00:26:20.020
it became impractical
to manage by hand.

00:26:20.020 --> 00:26:24.560
And so we were
using the API more.

00:26:24.560 --> 00:26:27.730
But when I first started
playing with BigQuery,

00:26:27.730 --> 00:26:29.460
it was just with the interface.

00:26:29.460 --> 00:26:33.867
And Felipe-- he is actually
using some of his databases.

00:26:33.867 --> 00:26:35.700
He just sent me some
links to his databases,

00:26:35.700 --> 00:26:38.540
and they showed
up on my account,

00:26:38.540 --> 00:26:41.100
and we just started
playing with them.

00:26:41.100 --> 00:26:42.788
Yeah.

00:26:42.788 --> 00:26:45.310
And then, yeah, just clicking
on tables-- and you can click,

00:26:45.310 --> 00:26:46.850
see all the columns
in the schema,

00:26:46.850 --> 00:26:50.215
and just writing SQL, and
looking around at the data.

00:26:50.215 --> 00:26:51.090
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:26:51.090 --> 00:26:53.786
So I'm quite curious to know
what the process was for coming

00:26:53.786 --> 00:26:54.785
up with those questions.

00:26:54.785 --> 00:26:56.270
NICK TZAPERAS: [LAUGHS]

00:26:56.270 --> 00:26:57.360
MARK MANDEL: I know
you had the data sets.

00:26:57.360 --> 00:26:58.740
Did they, like,
pose the question

00:26:58.740 --> 00:26:59.880
to you, and then
you were, like, OK,

00:26:59.880 --> 00:27:01.930
now I have to answer this
question, using the BigQuery,

00:27:01.930 --> 00:27:04.596
and then you'd go off and answer
it and just rinse and repeat?

00:27:04.596 --> 00:27:05.657
Or what happened?

00:27:05.657 --> 00:27:06.490
NICK TZAPERAS: Yeah.

00:27:06.490 --> 00:27:08.031
That was the most
challenging thing--

00:27:08.031 --> 00:27:10.530
was coming up with questions,
because the data sets

00:27:10.530 --> 00:27:13.190
themselves aren't that
exciting, honestly.

00:27:13.190 --> 00:27:18.020
It's just Reddit comments,
and GitHub commit pushes.

00:27:18.020 --> 00:27:21.260
So trying to create a question
that would make you laugh

00:27:21.260 --> 00:27:24.292
but was also still
possible to actually query

00:27:24.292 --> 00:27:25.250
was pretty challenging.

00:27:25.250 --> 00:27:28.260
And the process evolved a lot.

00:27:28.260 --> 00:27:33.580
At first, we had a writer
that was just coming up

00:27:33.580 --> 00:27:36.870
with fun questions,
and a strategist--

00:27:36.870 --> 00:27:38.990
they were both working on
just writing questions,

00:27:38.990 --> 00:27:40.994
and then I would
go and see which

00:27:40.994 --> 00:27:42.910
of the questions that
they wrote were actually

00:27:42.910 --> 00:27:46.660
possible to search.

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:49.706
And this is before I even really
knew the data sets that well.

00:27:49.706 --> 00:27:52.390
And so I'd take the questions
and have to actually go

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:54.270
see-- is this queryable?

00:27:54.270 --> 00:27:56.220
I'm not sure-- and go
try it out and realize,

00:27:56.220 --> 00:27:57.960
oh, no, there's actually, kind
of, a limited things that we

00:27:57.960 --> 00:27:58.920
can search.

00:27:58.920 --> 00:28:03.073
So then, we changed it up to
where we reversed it towards,

00:28:03.073 --> 00:28:05.410
like, OK, we're more
familiar with the data sets.

00:28:05.410 --> 00:28:07.170
Here's what we can query.

00:28:07.170 --> 00:28:08.490
Try to make these interesting.

00:28:08.490 --> 00:28:09.156
MARK MANDEL: Ah.

00:28:09.156 --> 00:28:10.350
I see.

00:28:10.350 --> 00:28:12.641
NICK TZAPERAS: I think the
one that we had the most fun

00:28:12.641 --> 00:28:16.580
with was pulling tags
from Freebase, which is,

00:28:16.580 --> 00:28:20.590
kind of, like a big database.

00:28:20.590 --> 00:28:23.230
It's just a bunch of
connected-- you can go from,

00:28:23.230 --> 00:28:25.650
like, Tom Cruise was
an actor in this movie,

00:28:25.650 --> 00:28:28.360
and this movie was
done in 1983, and then

00:28:28.360 --> 00:28:29.948
1983 is the year
that this happened.

00:28:29.948 --> 00:28:32.007
It's just this big,
connected graph database.

00:28:32.007 --> 00:28:34.340
And there are certain tags
in there, so you can pull up,

00:28:34.340 --> 00:28:37.500
like-- we have one question
about which 80s movie was

00:28:37.500 --> 00:28:39.870
talked about most on
/r/movies last year.

00:28:39.870 --> 00:28:40.703
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Mm.

00:28:40.703 --> 00:28:41.470
Nice.

00:28:41.470 --> 00:28:43.470
NICK TZAPERAS: And so we
were able to get, like,

00:28:43.470 --> 00:28:47.020
movies that were made
in the 80s, musicians,

00:28:47.020 --> 00:28:49.900
food ingredients, breeds of
dogs, and things like that.

00:28:49.900 --> 00:28:53.350
And so I think discovering that
made a lot of fun questions,

00:28:53.350 --> 00:28:56.450
because then, you're
able to just search--

00:28:56.450 --> 00:28:59.460
a lot of these databases
like Reddit are just text.

00:28:59.460 --> 00:29:00.770
It's just people typing text.

00:29:00.770 --> 00:29:03.770
There's no structure, and
so getting a structured data

00:29:03.770 --> 00:29:08.650
set, and then being able to
search for certain categories

00:29:08.650 --> 00:29:11.756
within Reddit-- that, I think,
created a lot of fun questions.

00:29:11.756 --> 00:29:14.255
MARK MANDEL: The combination
of data sets really [INAUDIBLE]

00:29:14.255 --> 00:29:15.502
be able to do some
interesting things.

00:29:15.502 --> 00:29:16.335
NICK TZAPERAS: Yeah.

00:29:16.335 --> 00:29:18.720
Which was also tricky, because
that creates a huge query.

00:29:18.720 --> 00:29:22.305
Because you're, kind of, pulling
all of these movies over here,

00:29:22.305 --> 00:29:24.430
searching all of these
comments over here for them,

00:29:24.430 --> 00:29:25.650
and it just multiplies.

00:29:25.650 --> 00:29:28.120
You know, you have, like,
millions of comments in Reddit

00:29:28.120 --> 00:29:30.010
that you're searching
for, and thousands

00:29:30.010 --> 00:29:33.429
of movies you're searching for
within each individual comment.

00:29:33.429 --> 00:29:35.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Because
something important to mention

00:29:35.720 --> 00:29:38.124
is the fact that you really
don't know what people are

00:29:38.124 --> 00:29:39.290
going to write as an answer.

00:29:39.290 --> 00:29:42.380
So you cannot even cast
a solution or anything.

00:29:42.380 --> 00:29:45.220
So it's like, every single time
that someone answers something,

00:29:45.220 --> 00:29:47.276
you're actually processing
the whole millions

00:29:47.276 --> 00:29:48.150
and millions of data.

00:29:48.150 --> 00:29:48.560
NICK TZAPERAS: Yeah.

00:29:48.560 --> 00:29:48.720
No.

00:29:48.720 --> 00:29:50.970
That's exactly what we want
to do with the experience.

00:29:50.970 --> 00:29:53.660
There was some talk about doing
a multiple-choice versus typing

00:29:53.660 --> 00:29:55.451
in an answer, and we
definitely, definitely

00:29:55.451 --> 00:29:59.050
wanted to try having
users type in an answer.

00:29:59.050 --> 00:30:01.660
Because then, you get
that feel of actually

00:30:01.660 --> 00:30:03.400
hitting BigQuery live.

00:30:03.400 --> 00:30:05.060
And you can type in
whatever you want,

00:30:05.060 --> 00:30:07.530
and it'll go search for
it, versus multiple-choice.

00:30:07.530 --> 00:30:11.349
If you have like five
choices and you choose E,

00:30:11.349 --> 00:30:13.140
you're, kind of,
thinking, well, maybe they

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:15.940
pulled those answers yesterday,
and it took five hours

00:30:15.940 --> 00:30:16.810
to run the query.

00:30:16.810 --> 00:30:16.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:30:16.900 --> 00:30:17.950
You can totally fake it.

00:30:17.950 --> 00:30:18.880
NICK TZAPERAS: Yeah, you
can, kind of, fake it.

00:30:18.880 --> 00:30:21.710
But I think it turned out
great, where people can actually

00:30:21.710 --> 00:30:23.720
tell-- when they
type in the answer

00:30:23.720 --> 00:30:26.410
and they can type in absolutely
whatever they want, it shows

00:30:26.410 --> 00:30:29.280
the SQL, and the thing
he types in is in there,

00:30:29.280 --> 00:30:31.300
and then goes and
runs the query live.

00:30:31.300 --> 00:30:33.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I've got
to say, I actually got

00:30:33.300 --> 00:30:36.180
a question which was, what
was the most popular kitchen

00:30:36.180 --> 00:30:39.580
utensil during the last
month on Wikipedia.

00:30:39.580 --> 00:30:42.930
And I went with spork, which
is my favorite kitchen utensil.

00:30:42.930 --> 00:30:44.580
And the query actually worked.

00:30:44.580 --> 00:30:45.397
I got a zero.

00:30:45.397 --> 00:30:46.890
NICK TZAPERAS: Oh. [LAUGHTER]

00:30:46.890 --> 00:30:47.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So yeah, I don't

00:30:47.570 --> 00:30:49.006
think it was prepared for.

00:30:49.006 --> 00:30:50.690
Like, I don't think
that lots of people

00:30:50.690 --> 00:30:52.230
think about sporks very often.

00:30:52.230 --> 00:30:53.720
MARK MANDEL: Apart from
you, quite clearly.

00:30:53.720 --> 00:30:54.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:30:54.110 --> 00:30:54.750
I like sporks.

00:30:54.750 --> 00:30:55.640
NICK TZAPERAS: I don't
think I've ever cooked

00:30:55.640 --> 00:30:56.430
with-- I've used a spork.

00:30:56.430 --> 00:30:58.820
I don't think I've cooked
with one, though. [CHUCKLE]

00:30:58.820 --> 00:30:59.736
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:30:59.736 --> 00:31:02.514
So I have a couple questions
about your questions.

00:31:02.514 --> 00:31:06.360
The first one is, what
is your favorite question

00:31:06.360 --> 00:31:08.750
that you've written?

00:31:08.750 --> 00:31:11.510
NICK TZAPERAS: I think it was
the comic book character one.

00:31:11.510 --> 00:31:14.604
There was a lot of-- I mean,
there are so many of them.

00:31:14.604 --> 00:31:15.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:31:15.520 --> 00:31:17.940
NICK TZAPERAS: We're pulling
the database, finding

00:31:17.940 --> 00:31:21.110
a list of all comic book
characters-- there are,

00:31:21.110 --> 00:31:22.490
like 6000 or something.

00:31:22.490 --> 00:31:26.160
And so it's, kind of,
fun to see random people

00:31:26.160 --> 00:31:28.844
come up and just type in some
random comic book character

00:31:28.844 --> 00:31:30.760
that I've never heard
of-- go and look them up

00:31:30.760 --> 00:31:35.270
and it's some side character in
some Marvel comic, or whatever.

00:31:35.270 --> 00:31:36.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That is cool.

00:31:36.520 --> 00:31:38.478
NICK TZAPERAS: There was
one guy, specifically.

00:31:38.478 --> 00:31:41.050
Me and John-- we were trying
to get people to play.

00:31:41.050 --> 00:31:42.386
We said, hey, you want to play?

00:31:42.386 --> 00:31:43.896
He said, no, no, no.

00:31:43.896 --> 00:31:44.800
We're, like, OK, OK.

00:31:44.800 --> 00:31:46.160
And then, we go up and we play.

00:31:46.160 --> 00:31:50.510
And he sees the first question
is the comic book character

00:31:50.510 --> 00:31:51.320
question.

00:31:51.320 --> 00:31:53.880
And he immediately says,
oh, no-- and he runs out--

00:31:53.880 --> 00:31:54.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Now, I want to play.

00:31:54.860 --> 00:31:55.290
[LAUGHTER]

00:31:55.290 --> 00:31:56.873
NICK TZAPERAS: He
thought it was going

00:31:56.873 --> 00:32:00.482
to be, like, maybe some kind of
Trivial Pursuit-type questions.

00:32:00.482 --> 00:32:02.940
And he saw the comic book one
and he immediately was, like,

00:32:02.940 --> 00:32:04.350
oh, Batman.

00:32:04.350 --> 00:32:06.395
You know, he was ready to go.

00:32:06.395 --> 00:32:07.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:32:07.550 --> 00:32:10.895
What about-- what was the
hardest query to write?

00:32:10.895 --> 00:32:13.680
What was the question that
came with the hardest query?

00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:17.420
NICK TZAPERAS: I
think I would say

00:32:17.420 --> 00:32:18.670
it's the ones that we dropped.

00:32:18.670 --> 00:32:21.230
There were a few questions
that I was working really hard

00:32:21.230 --> 00:32:26.180
to get right, and it
was just the data itself

00:32:26.180 --> 00:32:28.360
that wasn't quite working out.

00:32:28.360 --> 00:32:30.545
So there was a few,
like, top musician

00:32:30.545 --> 00:32:32.470
questions, which I
think is in there,

00:32:32.470 --> 00:32:34.580
but we, kind of, tweaked it.

00:32:34.580 --> 00:32:37.240
That one-- I was trying to do
the thing where you could type

00:32:37.240 --> 00:32:40.684
in Kanye or Kanye
West or-- you could

00:32:40.684 --> 00:32:41.850
type in Lennon, John Lennon.

00:32:41.850 --> 00:32:44.950
There are a few aliases
for certain musicians

00:32:44.950 --> 00:32:47.080
that I want people to
be able to type in.

00:32:47.080 --> 00:32:50.870
And it was really slow, trying
to support all the aliases,

00:32:50.870 --> 00:32:55.240
and even some of the aliases
in the database were wrong.

00:32:55.240 --> 00:32:58.450
So there are many, many,
many, many different occasions

00:32:58.450 --> 00:33:01.520
where we're trying to
pull musicians or actors,

00:33:01.520 --> 00:33:04.440
and someone completely
unexpected comes up.

00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:06.440
So like, pulling top
musicians, John Cleese

00:33:06.440 --> 00:33:09.285
came up, because I think
he was in a musical.

00:33:09.285 --> 00:33:10.368
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, he was.

00:33:10.368 --> 00:33:11.196
He was in a musical.

00:33:11.196 --> 00:33:13.362
NICK TZAPERAS: And so,
technically, he's a musician.

00:33:13.362 --> 00:33:15.640
And then, he was the top
answer for the question.

00:33:15.640 --> 00:33:18.000
He was the most talked-about
musician, technically,

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:20.790
when we wrote this
query, in /r/music,

00:33:20.790 --> 00:33:25.334
because his alias is "friend" in
the database that we're using.

00:33:25.334 --> 00:33:27.000
And so whenever we
saw the word, friend,

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:29.760
it thought that they were
talking about John Cleese.

00:33:29.760 --> 00:33:31.600
And so he was, like,
number one, by far.

00:33:31.600 --> 00:33:35.005
He was, like, number one, John
Cleese, number two, Kanye West.

00:33:35.005 --> 00:33:37.640
I said, well, we're going
to have to figure out

00:33:37.640 --> 00:33:38.624
how to do this right.

00:33:38.624 --> 00:33:40.165
MARK MANDEL: I'm
sure John Cleese was

00:33:40.165 --> 00:33:41.081
very happy about that.

00:33:41.081 --> 00:33:41.767
[LAUGHTER]

00:33:41.767 --> 00:33:44.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah I guess
the biggest problem, then,

00:33:44.100 --> 00:33:47.440
was really just, like, yeah, you
need a dataset that is clean,

00:33:47.440 --> 00:33:49.027
and you have ambiguity there.

00:33:49.027 --> 00:33:49.860
NICK TZAPERAS: Yeah.

00:33:49.860 --> 00:33:51.610
Because a lot of times,
you don't actually

00:33:51.610 --> 00:33:53.220
know what-- I mean,
we're not doing

00:33:53.220 --> 00:33:57.160
natural language processing
on the Reddit comments.

00:33:57.160 --> 00:34:00.720
So it's trying to pull
data out of the comments

00:34:00.720 --> 00:34:03.190
where you know what they're
specifically talking about.

00:34:03.190 --> 00:34:06.550
So trying to come
up with questions

00:34:06.550 --> 00:34:09.290
or write questions to where
there's no ambiguity about what

00:34:09.290 --> 00:34:11.206
a user might be talking
about on Reddit-- that

00:34:11.206 --> 00:34:13.000
was the really challenging part.

00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:14.125
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:34:14.125 --> 00:34:16.049
Well, are there
any other questions

00:34:16.049 --> 00:34:17.090
that you'd like to share?

00:34:17.090 --> 00:34:19.870
Like, something that was really
cool and you'd like to share?

00:34:19.870 --> 00:34:23.469
NICK TZAPERAS: I think the
speed of it-- a lot of the data

00:34:23.469 --> 00:34:27.000
sets for GitHub, we're
doing all the time.

00:34:27.000 --> 00:34:29.040
So some of the
questions that got cut,

00:34:29.040 --> 00:34:32.170
just because I guess they
weren't quite family-friendly,

00:34:32.170 --> 00:34:36.960
was, like, what programming
language cusses the most.

00:34:36.960 --> 00:34:41.770
And there's a list of, maybe, 20
different cuss words that we're

00:34:41.770 --> 00:34:42.920
searching for with GitHub.

00:34:42.920 --> 00:34:45.389
And I think that was
the first I went to,

00:34:45.389 --> 00:34:46.800
because it was just so fun.

00:34:46.800 --> 00:34:48.679
Everyone at the
office was really

00:34:48.679 --> 00:34:49.989
having fun with that one.

00:34:49.989 --> 00:34:51.375
And also, the poop emoji one.

00:34:51.375 --> 00:34:53.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What
was the language?

00:34:53.000 --> 00:34:53.530
NICK TZAPERAS:
Oh, you know what?

00:34:53.530 --> 00:34:53.960
I think--

00:34:53.960 --> 00:34:55.543
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
want to say it's C,

00:34:55.543 --> 00:34:57.280
because it's so hard to write.

00:34:57.280 --> 00:34:57.780
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:57.780 --> 00:34:58.600
NICK TZAPERAS: C
was surprisingly--

00:34:58.600 --> 00:35:00.820
I think they were
surprisingly happy people.

00:35:00.820 --> 00:35:01.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:35:01.230 --> 00:35:01.710
Nice.

00:35:01.710 --> 00:35:02.190
MARK MANDEL: Java.

00:35:02.190 --> 00:35:02.650
I'm gonna go with Java.

00:35:02.650 --> 00:35:03.900
NICK TZAPERAS: I think it was.

00:35:03.900 --> 00:35:05.430
Java was up there.

00:35:05.430 --> 00:35:06.874
But it was the speed of it.

00:35:06.874 --> 00:35:08.540
Like, it went through
every single event

00:35:08.540 --> 00:35:11.450
that happened in GitHub for
the past, like, five years.

00:35:11.450 --> 00:35:13.810
Like, just commits,
pushes, issue comments,

00:35:13.810 --> 00:35:16.440
opening an issue, closing
an issue, like all of it--

00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:18.785
it went through all of
it in, like, 10 seconds.

00:35:18.785 --> 00:35:20.660
I mean, that's when I
first started using it.

00:35:20.660 --> 00:35:26.997
That was a big wow
moment-- just the speed

00:35:26.997 --> 00:35:28.330
that you have accessible to you.

00:35:28.330 --> 00:35:30.039
Because doing a Google
search, obviously,

00:35:30.039 --> 00:35:32.371
is super, super fast, and
that's always been impressive.

00:35:32.371 --> 00:35:34.370
But now that it's been
kind of turned around

00:35:34.370 --> 00:35:37.450
and now you have the ability
to use that infrastructure

00:35:37.450 --> 00:35:40.730
to search whatever you
want within seconds,

00:35:40.730 --> 00:35:42.230
that moment was
really, really cool.

00:35:42.230 --> 00:35:42.700
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:35:42.700 --> 00:35:43.825
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:35:43.825 --> 00:35:46.500
Well, thank you so much for
taking the time to talk to us.

00:35:46.500 --> 00:35:46.620
NICK TZAPERAS: Yeah.

00:35:46.620 --> 00:35:46.860
No problem.

00:35:46.860 --> 00:35:47.320
Thank you.

00:35:47.320 --> 00:35:47.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Thank you.

00:35:47.730 --> 00:35:49.049
MARK MANDEL: Thank you.

00:35:49.049 --> 00:35:51.590
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Thank you so
much to John, Violet, and Nick,

00:35:51.590 --> 00:35:54.210
for those amazing interviews.

00:35:54.210 --> 00:35:57.000
We have a bunch of links
in the Show Notes, too,

00:35:57.000 --> 00:36:01.480
so you can see pictures of
demos, videos, and so on.

00:36:01.480 --> 00:36:03.870
They're totally worth
having a look at,

00:36:03.870 --> 00:36:06.110
so just go there and enjoy.

00:36:06.110 --> 00:36:09.780
And now, we're going to go
for the question of the week.

00:36:09.780 --> 00:36:10.530
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:36:10.530 --> 00:36:11.820
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So the
question of the week

00:36:11.820 --> 00:36:13.240
is about Deployment Manager.

00:36:13.240 --> 00:36:15.250
And as I said,
it's the first time

00:36:15.250 --> 00:36:18.690
we talk about it at all, so
I think it's probably worth

00:36:18.690 --> 00:36:21.500
mentioning, a little bit,
what Deployment Manager is.

00:36:21.500 --> 00:36:23.000
MARK MANDEL: So
Deployment Manager

00:36:23.000 --> 00:36:27.110
is a way to programmatically
and declaratively, basically,

00:36:27.110 --> 00:36:29.180
say what infrastructure
you want to spin up

00:36:29.180 --> 00:36:30.510
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:36:30.510 --> 00:36:32.410
So maybe you want a
whole bunch of machines,

00:36:32.410 --> 00:36:33.951
maybe you want to
Kubernetes Cluster,

00:36:33.951 --> 00:36:36.800
or maybe you want a
Cloud SQL database.

00:36:36.800 --> 00:36:38.672
And so you can write
these YAML files,

00:36:38.672 --> 00:36:40.380
send them out through
Deployment Manager,

00:36:40.380 --> 00:36:42.980
and it will create that for
you and [INAUDIBLE] away.

00:36:42.980 --> 00:36:43.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:36:43.920 --> 00:36:47.820
So it's, kind of, like
Kubernetes configuration

00:36:47.820 --> 00:36:51.050
files, where you describe your
architecture of your cluster.

00:36:51.050 --> 00:36:55.660
But it's a little bit-- it's
a higher level, where you're

00:36:55.660 --> 00:36:59.280
describing the architecture
of your Google Cloud Platform

00:36:59.280 --> 00:37:00.360
project in general.

00:37:00.360 --> 00:37:01.270
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:37:01.270 --> 00:37:02.728
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is very cool.

00:37:02.728 --> 00:37:04.429
There's actually--
for some people

00:37:04.429 --> 00:37:06.220
that may have used it
before-- there's also

00:37:06.220 --> 00:37:09.100
Terraform by HashiCorp.

00:37:09.100 --> 00:37:10.380
It's, kind of, the same idea.

00:37:10.380 --> 00:37:12.690
Terraform works across
different platforms,

00:37:12.690 --> 00:37:17.900
and Cloud Deployment Manager is
just for Google Cloud Platform.

00:37:17.900 --> 00:37:20.251
So what was the question, then?

00:37:20.251 --> 00:37:22.500
MARK MANDEL: So the question
that got asked, actually,

00:37:22.500 --> 00:37:26.070
was by one of our Google
Cloud Platform users,

00:37:26.070 --> 00:37:29.800
who was asking how you parse
in variables to Deployment

00:37:29.800 --> 00:37:30.360
Manager.

00:37:30.360 --> 00:37:31.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Oh, interesting.

00:37:31.150 --> 00:37:32.480
MARK MANDEL: So if you're
writing your script,

00:37:32.480 --> 00:37:34.590
you've got, say, a YAML
file, but maybe you

00:37:34.590 --> 00:37:37.380
want three instances of
something in, like, testing,

00:37:37.380 --> 00:37:39.360
but you want 20
instances in production.

00:37:39.360 --> 00:37:40.899
How can you do that?

00:37:40.899 --> 00:37:42.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
how do you do it?

00:37:42.440 --> 00:37:43.773
MARK MANDEL: How do you do that?

00:37:43.773 --> 00:37:45.860
So it's actually
reasonably straightforward.

00:37:45.860 --> 00:37:48.410
One of the things that
Deployment Manager supports

00:37:48.410 --> 00:37:49.830
is a thing called, templates.

00:37:49.830 --> 00:37:51.731
And basically, they're
Jinja templates.

00:37:51.731 --> 00:37:54.230
If you've ever used Python,
it's a standard templating thing

00:37:54.230 --> 00:37:55.040
for Python.

00:37:55.040 --> 00:37:55.830
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, I know those.

00:37:55.830 --> 00:37:56.410
MARK MANDEL: You know those?

00:37:56.410 --> 00:37:56.910
Yeah?

00:37:56.910 --> 00:37:57.930
Wonderful.

00:37:57.930 --> 00:38:00.300
And so rather than just
writing a YAML file,

00:38:00.300 --> 00:38:03.840
you write a template with these
variable placeholders in them,

00:38:03.840 --> 00:38:05.273
and then you can populate them.

00:38:05.273 --> 00:38:07.770
Now, where it got a little
confusing for the person

00:38:07.770 --> 00:38:09.950
in question was, they
figured, oh, maybe I

00:38:09.950 --> 00:38:13.840
can parse in the variables
on the command line.

00:38:13.840 --> 00:38:16.640
But instead of doing that,
actually, what you do is,

00:38:16.640 --> 00:38:20.576
you end up doing a YAML file
where you import the template.

00:38:20.576 --> 00:38:22.200
And then, when you
import the template,

00:38:22.200 --> 00:38:24.692
you use that import
as a custom resource.

00:38:24.692 --> 00:38:26.150
And then, when you
do that, you can

00:38:26.150 --> 00:38:27.910
parse in variables
inside that YAML file.

00:38:27.910 --> 00:38:30.220
So they just become, like,
child elements within the YAML.

00:38:30.220 --> 00:38:32.400
We'll have it in the Show Notes,
so you can see the examples.

00:38:32.400 --> 00:38:34.535
But the templates are
super, super powerful

00:38:34.535 --> 00:38:36.660
because you have the full
power of Jinja behind it.

00:38:36.660 --> 00:38:38.830
And then, you can simply just
parse in whatever variables

00:38:38.830 --> 00:38:40.310
you need within
that configuration.

00:38:40.310 --> 00:38:42.020
So you might have a
test configuration

00:38:42.020 --> 00:38:43.770
for your testing
servers, and then, maybe,

00:38:43.770 --> 00:38:45.600
a prod version of
that as well for when

00:38:45.600 --> 00:38:47.742
you want the 20, rather
than the 3, 4, or 5.

00:38:47.742 --> 00:38:49.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is very cool.

00:38:49.200 --> 00:38:51.640
I'm looking at some of the
configuration files now,

00:38:51.640 --> 00:38:55.070
and, yeah, they actually remind
me a lot of queue configs.

00:38:55.070 --> 00:38:56.280
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:38:56.280 --> 00:38:58.170
If you look at the
configuration files,

00:38:58.170 --> 00:38:59.944
they match up exactly
with the APIs--

00:38:59.944 --> 00:39:02.610
the REST APIs that we have to be
able to create stuff and delete

00:39:02.610 --> 00:39:03.640
stuff.

00:39:03.640 --> 00:39:05.169
So it's directly
in line with that.

00:39:05.169 --> 00:39:06.710
They're pretty
straightforward to use

00:39:06.710 --> 00:39:08.140
once you get the hang of them.

